[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The network is healthy; nothing is being “swallowed” by super_spine_router_0. End-to-end tests show pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) can reach pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) with 0% loss and ~3–4 ms RTT in both directions (topology-wide reachability test). Control-plane state confirms the normal Clos forwarding path leaf→spine→leaf is in use and does not require the super-spine for this east–west flow.

Localization and mechanism:
- leaf_router_0_0 has 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.4 on eth0 (toward spine_router_0_0).
- spine_router_0_0 has 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.7 (toward leaf_router_0_1), and symmetric routes for 10.0.0.0/24 via leaf_router_0_0.
- leaf_router_0_1 directly serves 10.0.1.0/24.
- super_spine_router_0 has valid BGP routes to both 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.1 (to spine_router_0_0) and is not on the shortest data path between the two leaves. There is no evidence of blackholing.

Root cause: No network fault; the reported symptom stems from a misunderstanding of the Clos topology—traffic between the two leaf-connected hosts transits the spine layer and does not traverse the super-spine.

Corrective fix:
No changes required. Close the incident. For verification or education, you can traceroute from pc_0_0 to pc_0_1 to observe the leaf→spine→leaf path and confirm the super-spine is bypassed for this flow.
